Unless I am mistaken, these two types of points of $\mathfrak X$ (which exhaust \deleted{everything} if $n=1$, \inserted{which is the only case, along with $n=0$, where $\mathfrak X$ is a scheme!}) are the only ones for which $\underline{O}_{\mathfrak X,x}$ is noetherian. However, we have

\margin{$\big|$}
\uline{Proposition} For $\forall x\in\mathfrak X$, $\underline{O}_{\mathfrak X,x}$ is a valuation ring in $L$, with field of fractions $L$, and containing $K$. The map $x\longmapsto\underline{O}_{\mathfrak X,x}$ is a bijection from $\mathfrak X$ onto the set of these rings in $L$.
\margin{One could start from an excellent scheme $X$, and construct a relative $\mathfrak X$ over it\ldots}

\uline{Proof} a) Let $f\in L\setminus\underline{O}_{\mathfrak X,x}$, we must prove $\frac1f\in\underline{O}_{\mathfrak X,x}$. Now $f=g/h$, \inserted{$g,h$}$\in\underline{O}_{X,a}$, where $X$ a \inserted{regular} model (proper), $a$ a point, $f,g$ \deleted{relatively prime} --- using resolution, \deleted{\unsure{we} \uline{\unsure{separate}} \unsure{the divisors}} \inserted{we may assume $\exists$ a regular \unsure{system}} of parameters, with $f,g$ monomials
\margin{121}
